  #set text(font: "Palatino Linotype", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.49pt, 125.78pt, "2.3. Orange Data Mining Application and Image Management ")
  #set text(font: "Palatino Linotype", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(187.73pt, 141.62pt, "Orange is a machine learning and data mining suite for data analysis through Python ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 154.46pt, "version 3.12.6 scripting and visual programming [29]. Orange is open-source data mining ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 167.18pt, "software version 3.37.0 for visualizing, analyzing, and modeling data.  ")
  #line-at(187.73pt, 180.02pt, "Several authors, such as Mohapatra and Swarnkar [43], have used Orange Data Min-")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 192.86pt, "ing to compare different artificial intelligence techniques. Ishak et al. [44] used the soft-")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 205.7pt, "ware in the classification method on the dataset lenses. Tebala and Marino [45] classified ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 218.54pt, "sector of economy activity. ")
  #line-at(187.73pt, 231.26pt, "Orange provides a graphical user interface that allows users to create data mining ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 244.1pt, "workflows by dragging and dropping widgets onto a palette. Widgets can include a sim-")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 256.94pt, "ple data table, graph, machine learning model, or custom interface. ")
  #line-at(187.73pt, 269.81pt, "The software can perform tasks such as data preprocessing, classification, regression, ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 282.65pt, "clustering, network analysis, data visualization, and more. It also supports many data file ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 295.49pt, "formats, including CSV, Excel, SQL, and others.  ")
  #line-at(187.73pt, 308.21pt, "The Orange Data Mining interface has widgets associated with different actions, such ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 321.05pt, "as data manipulation, transform, and model, which can be seen in the image analytics. ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 333.89pt, "The image embedding widget connects to the Test and Score widget to obtain results of ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 346.73pt, "testing classification algorithms. The widget does two things. First, it shows a table with ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 359.57pt, "different classifier performance measures, such as classification accuracy and area under ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 372.29pt, "the curve. Second, it outputs evaluation results, which can be used by other widgets for ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 385.13pt, "analyzing the performance of classifiers, as shown in Figure 4. ")
  #set text(font: "Palatino Linotype", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.49pt, 688.06pt, "Figure 4. ")
  #set text(font: "Palatino Linotype", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(204.41pt, 688.06pt, "Connecting to the Test and Score widget. ")
  #set text(font: "Palatino Linotype", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(187.73pt, 712.54pt, "It is necessary to use models such as logistic regression, which is a widely used sta-")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 725.38pt, "tistical method, for modeling the probability of a binary outcome based on one or more ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 738.22pt, "predictor variables. Unlike linear regression, which predicts a continuous outcome, lo-")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 750.936pt, "gistic regression is used when the dependent variable is categorical and typically binary ")
  #line-at(166.49pt, 763.776pt, "(0 or 1, success or failure, respectively) [46]. The core idea of logistic regression is to model ")
]

